# Contributing to {{ title }}

## The gate

```console
$ cargo fmt --check && cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings && cargo test --workspace
```

The same commands CI runs, so a green terminal means a green pull request.

The tests in `crates/{{ name }}/tests/` run the built binary the way a user
does. One of them, `installers.rs`, reads the two installers rather than
running them: it holds them to what the line learned the hard way - a Windows
shell running the Unix script is sent to the PowerShell one, and both name a
crate that exists.

`rust-version` is a promise to anyone running `cargo install`. A CI job builds
on exactly that version, so the number cannot quietly rot.

## Layout

| Path | What lives there |
| --- | --- |
| `crates/{{ name }}-core/` | The library: everything the tool knows how to do |
| `crates/{{ name }}/` | The binary: argument parsing and printing, nothing else |
| `crates/{{ name }}/tests/` | Tests that run the built binary, as a user would |
| `crates/{{ name }}/assets/icon.ico` | The icon the Windows binary embeds |
| `assets/` | The mark's three master SVGs |
| `tools/` | The installers the README points at |
| `npm/` | The npm package: a launcher that fetches the release binary |
| `docs/adr/` | Architecture decisions, newest last |

Both crates share one version, set in `[workspace.package]`: they are released
together, and a version that only applies to half a repository is a version
nobody can reason about.

The `.ico` lives inside the binary's crate because `cargo install` builds from
the published crate, and a crate carries only what is under its own directory:
an icon reached through `../../assets` builds here and is missing from
crates.io. The mark itself is the line's umbrella mark - lambda on graphite -
until the product has one of its own; `lyrn.toml` says `mark = "placeholder"`
meanwhile. The `.ico` is drawn from the masters by `docs/export-assets.mjs`,
which comes with the documentation site (`lyrn new {{ name }} --form docs`).
